  "mechanismSummary": "Borane adds H and B across the C=C in one four-centre step, so they end up on the same face (syn), with boron on the less substituted carbon. The hydroperoxide anion adds to boron, and the carbon moves from boron to oxygen keeping its configuration (retention). Hydrolysis frees the alkoxide. H and OH have therefore added syn, and the methyl ends up trans to OH: racemic trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol. One B–H is drawn reacting; BH₃ really uses all three.",
